WEBVTT

1
00:00:13.569 --> 00:00:20.822
Mrs. Fairfax turned out to be exactly as she appeared: a placid woman of average intelligence.

2
00:00:20.824 --> 00:00:26.655
I was thankful for the kindness she showed me, and I took pleasure in spending time with her.

3
00:00:26.656 --> 00:00:37.238
Adele turned out to be a lively, spoiled child. She had no extraordinary talents, but she made reasonable progress with her studies.

4
00:00:37.239 --> 00:00:43.650
Her efforts to please me inspired affection, and so we were content in each other's company.

5
00:00:43.651 --> 00:00:54.152
But sometimes when I went down to the gates and looked out at the road or climbed up to the roof and gazed out over the fields, I hungered for something more.

6
00:00:54.153 --> 00:01:04.535
I valued what was good in Adele and Mrs. Fairfax, but I longed to meet a greater variety of people and to see places that I had only heard of.

7
00:01:04.536 --> 00:01:12.171
Restlessness was in my nature. Women need to keep their minds active as much as men do.

8
00:01:12.172 --> 00:01:20.054
It is thoughtless to condemn them if they seek to do more or learn more than custom has deemed appropriate for their sex.

9
00:01:20.054 --> 00:01:24.502
My sole relief was to walk along the third-floor corridor.

10
00:01:24.503 --> 00:01:31.739
Safe in the silence and solitude, I let my mind dwell on whatever bright visions rose before it.

11
00:01:31.740 --> 00:01:42.490
When I was alone there, I often heard Grace Poole's laugh. I also heard her eccentric murmurs, which were even stranger than her laugh.

12
00:01:42.491 --> 00:01:48.338
Sometimes she would come out of her room with a plate or tray and go down to the kitchen.

13
00:01:48.339 --> 00:01:55.223
I made a few attempts to draw her into conversation, but her short replies ended my efforts.

14
00:01:55.224 --> 00:01:59.158
October, November, and December passed.

15
00:01:59.159 --> 00:02:10.917
One day in January, we didn't have lessons because Adele was feeling ill. It was a fine, cold day, and I grew tired of sitting inside.

16
00:02:10.917 --> 00:02:17.153
Mrs. Fairfax had written a letter, so I offered to carry it to the post office in Hay.

17
00:02:17.154 --> 00:02:22.658
The distance—two miles—would make a pleasant late-afternoon walk.

18
00:02:22.659 --> 00:02:25.822
It was uphill all the way to Hay.

19
00:02:25.823 --> 00:02:35.299
When I was about halfway there, I stopped to rest on a fence. From my seat I could look down on the gray house in the distance.

20
00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:41.695
I lingered there until the sun went down among the trees and the moon began to rise.

21
00:02:41.696 --> 00:02:46.867
Suddenly I heard the sound of hoofbeats—a horse was coming.

22
00:02:46.868 --> 00:02:55.869
I remembered one of Bessie's tales about a spirit in the shape of a horse or large dog that sometimes haunted weary travelers.

23
00:02:55.869 --> 00:03:06.125
Just then a great black-and-white dog rushed silently past me! The horse followed, a tall steed with a rider on its back.

24
00:03:06.126 --> 00:03:13.251
No one ever rode the creature in Bessie's tale, so this was no spirit, only a traveler.

25
00:03:13.252 --> 00:03:23.500
Horse and rider passed by, and I stood up. I had only gone a few steps when I heard a sliding sound and a clattering tumble.

26
00:03:23.501 --> 00:03:26.663
"What now?" cried the man.

27
00:03:26.664 --> 00:03:35.172
Man and horse were down on the ground, having slipped on the ice that glazed the road. The dog came bounding back.

28
00:03:35.173 --> 00:03:43.474
Seeing his master in a predicament and hearing the horse groan, the dog barked until the hills echoed with the sound.

29
00:03:43.475 --> 00:03:49.061
Then he ran up to me for help because I was the only person nearby.

30
00:03:49.062 --> 00:03:55.820
I followed the dog over to the traveler, who was struggling to work himself free from his horse.

31
00:03:55.821 --> 00:04:00.708
His efforts were so vigorous that I didn't think he could be badly hurt.

32
00:04:00.709 --> 00:04:02.870
"Are you injured, sir?"

33
00:04:02.871 --> 00:04:06.640
The man was still concentrating on freeing himself.

34
00:04:06.641 --> 00:04:11.394
I spoke again. "Can I do anything?"

35
00:04:11.394 --> 00:04:17.631
"You must stand aside," he said as he rose first to his knees and then to his feet.

36
00:04:17.632 --> 00:04:29.046
I did so, whereupon the horse began the heaving, stamping, clattering process of getting to its feet. All the while the dog barked and bayed.

37
00:04:29.047 --> 00:04:34.021
"Quiet, Pilot!" the man said, and the dog went silent.

38
00:04:34.022 --> 00:04:44.444
The traveler bent over and felt his own foot and leg. Apparently something was wrong because he now sat down on the fence that I had just left.

39
00:04:44.445 --> 00:04:46.576
I approached him again.

40
00:04:46.576 --> 00:04:54.008
"If you are hurt and want help, sir," I said, "I can fetch someone from Thornfield or from Hay."

41
00:04:54.009 --> 00:05:06.245
"Thank you, but I'll manage. I have no broken bones, only a sprain." He stood up and tried his foot again. "Ugh!" he groaned.

42
00:05:06.246 --> 00:05:17.740
Some daylight still lingered, and I could plainly see him. He had a dark face with stern features and black hair and looked to be in his mid-thirties.

43
00:05:17.741 --> 00:05:23.152
Had he been a handsome young gentleman, I wouldn't have dared speak to him.

44
00:05:23.153 --> 00:05:31.564
If he had cheerfully refused my offers of assistance, I would have gone on my way. But his frown made me stay.

45
00:05:31.565 --> 00:05:38.319
"I cannot think of leaving you at so late an hour on this lonely road until you're fit to ride your horse."

46
00:05:38.320 --> 00:05:43.810
He looked at me when I said this; he'd hardly turned his eyes in my direction before.

47
00:05:43.811 --> 00:05:49.633
"I'd think you should be at home yourself," he said. "Where do you come from?"

48
00:05:49.634 --> 00:05:54.447
"From just below, and I'm not afraid of being out late when there's moonlight."

49
00:05:54.448 --> 00:05:58.206
"Do you mean you live at Thornfield?"

50
00:05:58.207 --> 00:06:00.406
He pointed to the house.

51
00:06:00.406 --> 00:06:01.975
"Yes, sir."

52
00:06:01.976 --> 00:06:03.976
"Whose house is that?"

53
00:06:03.977 --> 00:06:07.131
"Mr. Rochester's," I replied.

54
00:06:07.132 --> 00:06:09.044
"Do you know him?"

55
00:06:09.045 --> 00:06:11.529
"No, I've never seen him."

56
00:06:11.530 --> 00:06:18.998
"You're not a servant at Thornfield, of course. You are . . ." He studied me intently.

57
00:06:18.999 --> 00:06:20.695
"The governess."

58
00:06:20.696 --> 00:06:32.010
"Ah! I'd forgotten about the governess," he said. "You may help me if you'll be so kind. Try to get hold of my horse's bridle and lead him to me."

59
00:06:32.011 --> 00:06:43.354
But try as I might, I couldn't catch the animal. And so with a slight apology, the man stood up, leaned heavily upon me, and limped over to his horse.

60
00:06:43.355 --> 00:06:51.174
He climbed into the saddle with a wince of pain. I handed him his whip, and off he rode.

61
00:06:51.175 --> 00:07:01.653
After taking the letter to Hay, I was reluctant to go home because I'd had this small adventure. To return to Thornfield was to return to stagnation.

62
00:07:01.654 --> 00:07:13.655
So I lingered at the gates, and then I lingered on the lawn, watching the moon and the stars. At last I had to turn away from the sky and enter the house.

63
00:07:13.656 --> 00:07:20.061
When I went to Mrs. Fairfax's room, no one was there except a great black-and-white dog.

64
00:07:20.062 --> 00:07:24.764
"Pilot?" I said, and the dog came to me.

65
00:07:24.765 --> 00:07:27.686
I rang the bell and Leah entered.

66
00:07:27.687 --> 00:07:30.708
"Whose dog is this?" I asked.

67
00:07:30.709 --> 00:07:43.142
"Mr. Rochester's," Leah replied. "John just went for the doctor because Mr. Rochester sprained his ankle. His horse fell on some ice."
